---
scope: src/app/learn/open-source-agentic-sdlc-framework
alignment: unreviewed
description: The /learn answer page for 'open source agentic sdlc framework' — intent-driven AIDE (aidemd) mapped onto the classic software lifecycle, with only the true open-source facts.
intent: >
  This page answers people searching for an open source agentic SDLC framework.
  It opens by saying plainly that AIDE (intent-driven, aidemd) is one and what
  that means, then shows which classic lifecycle phase each pipeline stage
  covers — requirements, design, implementation, testing, maintenance — and why
  intent specs keep agents aligned from the first spec through later changes.
  Its open-source claims are only the founder-verified facts, such as the MIT
  license, running locally and needing no account. A short FAQ gives
  quotable answers, and links lead on to the definition page, the subagents
  pipeline page and the docs.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The first paragraph answers the searched question directly — AIDE, paired with intent-driven or aidemd, is an open source agentic SDLC framework — and says what that means.
    - A reader can see which classic lifecycle phase each pipeline stage covers, with requirements, design, implementation, testing and maintenance all accounted for.
    - The page explains why intent specs keep agents aligned across the whole lifecycle, from the first spec through later changes.
    - Every open-source claim matches the founder-verified facts, such as the MIT license, running locally and needing no account.
    - A short FAQ gives definition-ready answers that a searcher or answer engine can quote one at a time.
    - Related links take the reader on to the definition page, the subagents pipeline page and the docs.
  undesired:
    - Copy aimed at the bare 'agentic sdlc' head term instead of the open-source framework phrasing searchers use.
    - A stage-to-phase mapping that leaves a classic phase unclaimed, or stretches a stage to claim a phase it does not really cover.
    - Open-source claims the verified facts do not back — a community size, adoption figure, hosted service or governance promise.
    - Agentic SDLC presented as one settled definition, or AIDE presented as the only open source option, when the term is still contested.
    - FAQ answers that only make sense read in order or lean on the surrounding page, so no single answer can be quoted alone.
    - A page that reads as a pitch rather than an explainer and leaves the reader no way on to the definition, pipeline or docs pages.
---
